  "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{\u2218}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{\u2218}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{\u2218}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{\u2218}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{\u2218}-{45}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={315}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {360}^{\u2218},\\,x={315}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {360}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
